WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.150
So it's Thursday and we do have new jobs numbers,
another one point three million Americans

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:09.100
have filed first time unemployment claims,
meaning last week.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.790
Remember this week we learned the numbers
from last week.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:16.500
Another one point three million Americans
have lost their jobs.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.600
Are you becoming numb to these numbers to
some degree?

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:24.900
I mean, I want to remind you, before the 15
week weeks in a row of corona virus job losses,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:30.539
the previous single day record for new unemployment
claims was about 700000.

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:35.579
We've had as many as six point six million
in one week and continue to have right under

00:00:35.579 --> 00:00:38.960
one point five million per week for several
weeks now.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.210
And the reason that this week is particularly
important is that today we got the numbers

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:44.960
from last week.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:51.680
In the last week, we have seen states have
to sort of re shut down elements of the economy.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:55.880
That's happening this week, meaning next week
and the week after.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:01.020
Are going to be crucial weeks in seeing whether
these numbers start to go up again.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.739
The rate at which Americans have been losing
their jobs has been coming down.

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:09.609
We're still seeing people lose jobs, but we
had six point six million one week and then

00:01:09.609 --> 00:01:11.209
five million, four million, etc.

00:01:11.209 --> 00:01:13.329
The rate has been coming down.

00:01:13.329 --> 00:01:18.359
Things are reclose thing, though, and the
next two weeks could be particularly ugly.

00:01:18.359 --> 00:01:25.490
Last week was more of a normal week, you might
say, as normal as things can be during a pandemic.

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:29.439
And one point three million more people lost
their jobs.

00:01:29.439 --> 00:01:33.630
So that's now fifteen straight weeks of job
losses.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:38.840
That's a total of 51 million jobless claims
since Corona virus started.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.479
Now, I know people e-mail me as if I'm trying
to deceive.

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:47.200
I'm mentioning we don't have 51 million people
out of work right now.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.329
So 51 million people at some point lost their
jobs during the last 15 weeks.

00:01:52.329 --> 00:01:55.329
Many millions of those have either gone back
to work or found new jobs.

00:01:55.329 --> 00:01:57.439
So of the 51 million.

00:01:57.439 --> 00:02:02.329
Only about 18 million remain actively unemployed
right now.

00:02:02.329 --> 00:02:07.280
And that's obviously far better than 51 million,
although still historically high.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.429
So now the question is, what happens next
week?

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:14.400
Corona virus has forced California to reclose
sectors of its economy.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.420
Indoor dining is done.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.159
Movie theater, a whole bunch of stuff that
had been open is now closing.

00:02:19.159 --> 00:02:22.980
I won't give you the full list of states,
but there are many that are having to go backwards

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.400
in terms of opening.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.769
Even Texas is is actually having to step back.

00:02:27.769 --> 00:02:33.329
So this week, which will be reported next
week and also the following week, are going

00:02:33.329 --> 00:02:34.810
to be very, very important.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:40.099
Now, the other thing that's upcoming and I
want to remind you about it is that on July

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:45.320
30th, we're going to get the first estimate
of second quarter GDP.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:52.190
Second quarter GDP has been widely expected
by both the left and the right to be maybe

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:55.379
the worst economic quarter for the United
States.

00:02:55.379 --> 00:02:57.650
Possibly, ever, possibly.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:04.000
We might get the worst GDP number in 14 days
that we have ever seen in the United States.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.150
The second quarter, of course, was April,
May, June.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:10.300
At the end of June, some stuff was starting
to reopen.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.060
A lot of the opening has happened in July.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:18.150
So at the end of this month, we're going to
get an estimate of just how bad was second

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.159
quarter GDP.

00:03:20.159 --> 00:03:23.180
I don't know how bad it's going to be.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:26.099
The expectation is that it's going to be quite
bad.

00:03:26.099 --> 00:03:31.180
And sometimes when you get a bad number, that's
slightly better than what was expected.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:36.799
It actually feels like, hey, we actually got
off less seriously than than we might have.

00:03:36.799 --> 00:03:39.290
So I don't know what the answer is going to
be.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:45.189
But in terms of the timing, July 30th, we're
going to get an estimate of Q2 GDP.

00:03:45.189 --> 00:03:49.030
August 30th is when we'll get a more fine
tuned number.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:56.370
And that is going to be very, very close to
the election of either Joe Biden or Donald

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.629
Trump for another four years.

00:03:58.629 --> 00:04:02.720
And this is exactly as we've been predicting
for a couple of months.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:04.049
Case is going up.

00:04:04.049 --> 00:04:08.549
Q to GDP number is going to be dropped on
top of us sometime in the next two to six

00:04:08.549 --> 00:04:13.859
weeks with different specificity and precision
as we get more accurate numbers by August

00:04:13.859 --> 00:04:14.859
30th.

00:04:14.859 --> 00:04:19.970
We are going to have dramatically more information
than we have right now about what the end

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:25.000
of the year and early 2021 is likely to be
for the United States.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.340
We may also have some vaccine news.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:29.240
We may also have news about therapeutics.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.090
So in my mind, I'm sorry.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:36.590
You know, I have a question on Friday's show
this week about where do I believe the point

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:40.930
of no return is for Donald Trump in terms
of if he doesn't turn his polling around and

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:43.790
I'm going to make the case that it's October
1st.

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:50.180
However, I'm seeing September 1st as a date,
at which point we are likely to have a lot

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:53.759
of additional important information about
how things are going.

00:04:53.759 --> 00:06:09.709
Economic data, case data and much, much more.

